From 9a9fd08a38bc83d6c3fc2df4dbc0a0c587fbd651 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anuj Gupta <anuj20.g@samsung.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 14:48:29 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 01/17] dma-buf: document dma_buf_io_map::seg_shift

The file I/O infrastructure added in patch 1 of "Add dmabuf read/write
via io_uring" declares seg_shift with no explanation of what it means,
who sets it, or why it exists. Christoph asked about this on v5 and it
was never answered.

It is the guaranteed minimum DMA segment size in the mapping, expressed
as a power-of-2 shift: drivers compute it as the minimum of
__ffs(sg_dma_len()) across the scatterlist, so every segment is at least
1U << seg_shift bytes and a multiple of that size.

That is what makes the block layer's segment bound work. A dma-buf bio
reports a single segment to the block layer, so bio_split_io_at_dmabuf()
cannot count segments by walking the mapping; instead it bounds the
transfer arithmetically:

	bytes <= (lim->max_segments << seg_shift) - offset

Without seg_shift a fragmented mapping could produce far more descriptor
entries than the driver's fixed-size pool holds.

Document all of that at the field, including that ->map() must set it.

No functional change.

Signed-off-by: Anuj Gupta <anuj20.g@samsung.com>
---
 include/linux/dma-buf-io.h | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/include/linux/dma-buf-io.h b/include/linux/dma-buf-io.h
index f31c7375ae91..d72182acf2ed 100644
--- a/include/linux/dma-buf-io.h
+++ b/include/linux/dma-buf-io.h
@@ -34,6 +34,23 @@ struct dma_buf_io_map {
 	 * is deferred until all refs are dropped.
 	 */
 	struct percpu_ref		refs;
+
+	/*
+	 * Guaranteed minimum DMA segment size in this mapping, as a
+	 * power-of-2 shift: every segment is at least 1U << seg_shift bytes
+	 * and a multiple of that size. Drivers compute it as the minimum of
+	 * __ffs(sg_dma_len()) across the scatterlist; see
+	 * nvme_dma_buf_io_map(). Must be set by ->map().
+	 *
+	 * A dma-buf bio reports a single segment to the block layer, so this
+	 * is what lets bio_split_io_at_dmabuf() (block/blk-merge.c) bound the
+	 * transfer to lim->max_segments without walking the mapping:
+	 *
+	 *	bytes <= (lim->max_segments << seg_shift) - offset
+	 *
+	 * Without it a fragmented mapping could produce far more descriptor
+	 * entries than the driver's fixed-size pool holds.
+	 */
 	unsigned			seg_shift;
 
 	struct work_struct		release_work;
